Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||04-13-99||02:27 PM||||the car I have in mind for this is a V8 Fiero based (obviously!) kit car..<p>Can anyone tell me where I can find some info on "autotronic" shifters? Some Dodge Intrepids use something similar, the AutoStick. I believe a Porsche model uses one with a different name, as do race cars. Also called clutchless manuals, "happy slappy"s, etc.<p>Can these type of shifters be used with any engine, or do they need to have an engine designed to be used with them?
Z||000001||Patrick||04-13-99||02:40 PM||||Check out the thread, "<b>Clutch Info uncovered...</b>". This is just what we've been talking about. There are many of us Fiero owners out here with "clutchless" manual transmissions. <p>I'm sorry, the stress of trying to shift gears in this car is getting to me. :)
Z||000002||theogre||04-14-99||09:15 AM||||You're a sick, sick, boy Patrick. :}<p>In the Intrepids case, I think the autostick is a semi manual controled automatic. Many inline TH chevy, and C series Ford, tranies get this treatment for racing. The valve body is altered so it only shifts on driver command. Autostick, as I understand it, is a cross between the full AOD, and a shift on demand race box.<p>Other systems, like that used in some older VW products actually released the clutch by pressing down the stick, they were other wise unmodified manual tranies.<p>Doing something like changing gears w/o a clutch, other than when stopped, requires major redesign of the manual tranny. for example Race tranies use by formula 1, or indy, don't use syncro's to avoid clutching, but they are otherwise manual gearboxes that are electrically shifted to avoid linkages, reduce driver fatigue, and eliminate the driver taking hands off the steering. These gear boxes are also cause of allot of breakdown in those classes.<p>There are several methods to acomplish this kind of a goal, the shifter generally has little or nothing to do with it.
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||04-14-99||12:33 PM||||Hmm. would it be possible to adapt *any* transmission like this to say, an LT-1?<br>(mmm.. V8 Fiero..)<p>I'd prefer something that has both normal automatic, and manual controlled automatic (aka AutoStick), but if that's not possible in a Fiero.. oh well. I don't want a normal manual or a normal auto.. but the AutoStick sounds like it'd be perfect for what I want.
Z||000004||theogre||04-14-99||05:59 PM||||I don't believe any auto tranny can be adapted to that behavior. You either have full auto, or driver commanded(manual)operation. Autostick is designed for this project from the ground up. That I'm aware of only Chrysler has it.<p>The autostick is a fully computer controled rig as well from what I understand. It has RPM limiting built into the system to keep it from over reving. I "normal" mode it's behavior is full AOD. In the "stick" mode, it's the same as any SOD inline used for drag racing, except that it's computer controlled. (This tranny would not be legal in most classes of NHRA from what one of my racing friends tells me. Current NHRA plans call for even more resticted shifting control, like the elimination of multi lever setups in many classes.) The key here is that the tranny is computer controlled. The shifter has little or no direct input to the tranny. In stick mode it is electrically shifted, with computer over ride to prevent over reving the motor. Even in stick mode the computer will force shifts if need be.
